// Regression test: `.` is instance-member-access only -- calling a static
// method (declared `fn StructName::method(...)`, no `self` at all) via `.`
// on the BARE STRUCT TYPE NAME (no instance) must be rejected. Only
// `StructName::method(...)` is valid.
//
// Before this fix, `.` silently worked here too: Resolver::visitMethodCall()
// had a separate legacy path (VariableExprAST::IsStructName) originally
// added only to turn a compiler CRASH (calling a method via a bare struct
// type name used to `report_fatal_error`) into a clean arity error -- never
// intended to make `.` an actual working way to invoke a method. But once
// true static methods existed (this session's own feature, `fn
// StructName::method(...)`, arity with no `self`), that old path's arity
// comparison (`ExpectedArity = IsStaticMethod ? ArgCount : ArgCount + 1`)
// accidentally matched them perfectly -- `MathUtil.square(5)` compiled and
// ran, computing the correct answer, identically to `MathUtil::square(5)`.
// Found by direct user request to actually verify the '.' vs '::' split is
// really enforced, not just assumed from the tests that happened to use
// '::' already.

struct MathUtil {}

fn MathUtil::square(x: int): int {
    return x * x;
}

fn main(): int {
    immut y: int = MathUtil.square(5);
    return 0;
}
